<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Costa Rica</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Costa Rica</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Costa Rica is a country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> to the north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> to the southeast, with coasts on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> Sea and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a>. Humans lived there from between 7,000 and 10,000 BCE, and various indigenous peoples were present before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> colonised the territory in the 16th century. It was a peripheral colony of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> until independence in 1821 as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-mexican-empire">First Mexican Empire</a>, then joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America</a> in 1823 and formally declared independence from it in 1847. After a brief civil war in 1948, the constitution of 1949 granted universal suffrage and permanently abolished the army.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Federal Republic of Central America emerged from the Captaincy General, or Kingdom, of Guatemala, the Spanish jurisdiction that stretched from Chiapas to <b>Costa Rica</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Recognition came only from Japan's allies and dependents, El Salvador, the Dominican Republic and <b>Costa Rica</b> in 1934, Italy, Spain, Germany and Hungary in 1937–1939, and from 1940 Axis satellites and Japanese dependents; the Soviet Union granted de jure recognition in the Neutrality Pact of 13 April 1941.</blockquote><a href="/empire/manchukuo">Manchukuo · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Morazán’s last attempt to restore the federation ended with his execution in <b>Costa Rica</b> in 1842, and conservative generals allied with Rafael Carrera of Guatemala, notably Francisco Ferrera, the first constitutional president, dominated the following years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Republic of Honduras · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The other Central American states, led by <b>Costa Rica</b> under Juan Rafael Mora, united against him in the National War: Nicaraguan forces defeated his men at the Battle of San Jacinto on 14 September 1856, and Walker surrendered at Rivas in May 1857.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A brief border war with <b>Costa Rica</b> over the Coto region in 1921 ended under American pressure in Costa Rica’s favour.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Republic of Panama · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Long-distance exchange brought Plumbate pottery from southern Guatemala and polychrome vessels from <b>Costa Rica</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/toltec-state">Toltec state · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
